 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A luminous object gives out its own light: the sun, stars, a fire, a candle flame, a lamp, a firefly. A non-luminous object does not; we see it because it reflects light: the moon, a book, a mirror.\nNatural sources: the sun, stars, lightning, fireflies. Artificial (man-made) sources: candles, oil lamps, torches, electric bulbs, LEDs.\nSome sources also give heat (a candle, a filament bulb); others give light with little heat (LEDs, fireflies). LEDs use less electricity for the same light.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Is the moon a source of light?",
  "steps": [
   "The moon does not make its own light.",
   "We see it because it reflects sunlight: it is non-luminous."
  ],
  "answer": "No: it is non-luminous; it reflects the sun's light.",
  "checks": []
 },
